ML_PAGE("l3-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l3\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">LECTURE 3 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Lecture 3 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 32 multiple-choice questions and 7 coding questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with answers hidden until you reveal them.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>32</strong><span>MCQs in the notes</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>7</strong><span>Coding questions</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Difficult-mode questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l3\" data-quiz=\"3\">Start Lecture 3 simulator</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l3\" id=\"l3p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">21 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li data-full=\"Lecture 3 — MCQ Exam Preparation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s0\" title=\"Lecture 3 — MCQ Exam Preparation\">Overview</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A — FUNDAMENTALS</li><li data-full=\"1. What you should know instantly\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s2\" title=\"1. What you should know instantly\"><span class=\"toc-no\">1</span>Key facts table</button></li><li data-full=\"2. Feature-engineering fundamentals\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s3\" title=\"2. Feature-engineering fundamentals\"><span class=\"toc-no\">2</span>Practice: fundamentals</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B — NUMERIC FEATURES</li><li data-full=\"3. Numeric engineering\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s5\" title=\"3. Numeric engineering\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Binarization and rounding</button></li><li data-full=\"4. Interaction features\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s6\" title=\"4. Interaction features\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Interaction features</button></li><li data-full=\"5. Polynomial features\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s7\" title=\"5. Polynomial features\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Polynomial features</button></li><li data-full=\"6. Binning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s8\" title=\"6. Binning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Binning</button></li><li data-full=\"7. Custom binning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s9\" title=\"7. Custom binning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>Custom bins</button></li><li data-full=\"8. Quantile binning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s10\" title=\"8. Quantile binning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>Quantile bins</button></li><li data-full=\"9. Log transformation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s11\" title=\"9. Log transformation\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Log transform</button></li><li data-full=\"10. Box-Cox\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s12\" title=\"10. Box-Cox\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>Box-Cox</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART C — CATEGORICAL FEATURES</li><li data-full=\"11. Nominal vs ordinal\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s14\" title=\"11. Nominal vs ordinal\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>Nominal vs ordinal</button></li><li data-full=\"12. Label encoding trap\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s15\" title=\"12. Label encoding trap\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>Label encoding trap</button></li><li data-full=\"13. One-hot encoding\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s16\" title=\"13. One-hot encoding\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>One-hot encoding</button></li><li data-full=\"14. Dummy coding\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s17\" title=\"14. Dummy coding\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>Dummy coding</button></li><li data-full=\"15. Effect coding\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s18\" title=\"15. Effect coding\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Effect coding</button></li><li data-full=\"16. Feature hashing\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s19\" title=\"16. Feature hashing\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Feature hashing</button></li><li data-full=\"17. Critical train/test rule\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s20\" title=\"17. Critical train/test rule\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>Fit encoders on train only</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART D — CODING &amp; REVIEW</li><li data-full=\"18. Lecture 3 coding questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s22\" title=\"18. Lecture 3 coding questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>Coding questions</button></li><li data-full=\"19. Lecture 3 answer key\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s23\" title=\"19. Lecture 3 answer key\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>Answer key</button></li><li data-full=\"20. Lecture 3 traps to drill\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"l3p-s24\" title=\"20. Lecture 3 traps to drill\"><span class=\"toc-no\">20</span>Top traps</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l3\" open><summary><div><strong>Lecture 3 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Know-cold tables, traps, practice questions and coding questions — section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>21 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s0\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Lecture 3 — MCQ Exam Preparation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Feature Engineering: Numeric &amp; Categorical Data</h4>\n<p>I’m treating <strong>Lecture 3</strong> as the first half of the combined slides: feature-engineering fundamentals, numeric features, and categorical features. This follows the natural structure of the material: the chapter first establishes feature engineering, then covers numeric data and categorical data before moving into text.</p>\n<p>The core idea to keep in your head throughout the exam is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Raw data} \\neq \\text{good features}}$$</div>\n<p>Feature engineering tries to represent the underlying problem in a form that the algorithm can learn from effectively. The lecture explicitly argues that good features can allow simpler models to outperform complex models working with poor features.</p></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l3p-d1\">PART A — FUNDAMENTALS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">1. What you should know instantly</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Concept</th>\n<th>Immediate thought</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature engineering</td>\n<td>Turn raw information into useful model features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Iterative process</td>\n<td>Engineer → Train → Evaluate → improve features</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Domain knowledge</td>\n<td>Helps decide meaningful transformations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Binarization</td>\n<td>Number/count → 0 or 1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rounding</td>\n<td>Remove meaningless precision</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interaction</td>\n<td>Combine features to expose joint effects</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Polynomial features</td>\n<td>$x_1,x_2,x_1^2,x_1x_2,x_2^2...$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Binning</td>\n<td>Continuous number → interval/category</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fixed-width binning</td>\n<td>Same numeric width</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Custom binning</td>\n<td>Domain-defined boundaries</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quantile binning</td>\n<td>Approximately equal observations per bin</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Log transform</td>\n<td>Compress large positive values/right skew</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Box-Cox</td>\n<td>Flexible power transformation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nominal</td>\n<td>Categories with no order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ordinal</td>\n<td>Categories with meaningful order</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Label encoding</td>\n<td>Category → integer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>One-hot</td>\n<td>$m$ categories → $m$ binary columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dummy coding</td>\n<td>$m$ categories → $m-1$ columns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Effect coding</td>\n<td>Reference category uses −1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Feature hashing</td>\n<td>Categories → fixed number of hash buckets</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">2. Feature-engineering fundamentals</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>What is the main purpose of feature engineering?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Increase the number of rows</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Transform raw data into useful representations for ML</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Always remove categorical features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Replace model evaluation</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>Which statement is most consistent with the lecture?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Complex algorithms always outperform simple algorithms</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Feature engineering is performed only once</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Good features can be more important than using a more complex algorithm</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Raw data should never be transformed</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>Which sequence best reflects the iterative feature-engineering process?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Engineer → Train → Evaluate → Refine</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Deploy → Delete → Encode → Train</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Evaluate → Collect → Stop</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Scale → Deploy → Ignore performance</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>Why is domain knowledge important?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It automatically removes all missing data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It helps identify transformations that make sense for the actual problem</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It makes test data unnecessary</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It replaces mathematics entirely</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l3p-d4\">PART B — NUMERIC FEATURES</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. Numeric engineering</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Binarization</h4>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>A music recommendation system only needs to know whether a user has ever listened to a song.</p>\n<p>Which transformation is most suitable?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Log transformation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Binarization</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Dummy coding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> TF-IDF</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>Given:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">listen_count = [0, 14, 0, 37, 2]\n</code></pre>\n<p>using threshold $&gt;0$, what is the binary result?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>[0, 14, 0, 37, 2]</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>[0, 1, 0, 1, 1]</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>[1, 0, 1, 0, 0]</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>[0, 0, 0, 1, 1]</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<h4>Rounding</h4>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>A sensor outputs:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$87.638294\\%$$</div>\n<p>but precision beyond the nearest whole percentage is meaningless.</p>\n<p>Which transformation is most appropriate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Binarization</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Rounding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> One-hot encoding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Hashing</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>Why might excessive decimal precision be harmful?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It may represent noise rather than meaningful signal</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Decimals cannot be processed by ML</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> All decimals indicate missing data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It forces unsupervised learning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. Interaction features</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>Suppose model performance depends not just on Attack and Defense separately, but on having both high simultaneously.</p>\n<p>Which feature could help?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Attack}+0$$</div></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Attack} \\times \\text{Defense}$$</div></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> One-hot encode Attack</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Drop Defense</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture uses exactly the interaction:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$49\\times49=2401$$</div>\n<p>to demonstrate this idea.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>Why create interaction features?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> To capture relationships between variables that may not be visible when treated independently</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> To reduce every feature to 0/1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> To remove categorical variables</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> To prevent test/train splitting</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. Polynomial features</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l3_poly_count\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Feature explosion</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · How quickly polynomial features multiply.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Given:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x_1,\\ x_2$$</div>\n<p>a degree-2 expansion may create:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$x_1,\\ x_2,\\ x_1^2,\\ x_1x_2,\\ x_2^2$$</div>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>What is a major danger of blindly applying polynomial features to many original columns?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Data becomes XML</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Feature explosion</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Categories become ordinal</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Missing values disappear</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture explicitly warns about feature explosion when polynomial expansion is applied to many columns.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. Binning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>Transforming:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Age}=37$$</div>\n<p>into:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">30–39\n</code></pre>\n<p>is called:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Scaling</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Binning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Stemming</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Hashing</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>Which best describes fixed-width bins?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Each bin contains equal numbers of observations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Each bin covers equal numerical width</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Boundaries are learned only by CNNs</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Every value becomes 0 or 1</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>Which is fixed-width?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">0–9\n10–19\n20–29\n30–39\n</code></pre></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <pre><code class=\"language-text\">Lowest 25%\nNext 25%\nNext 25%\nTop 25%\n</code></pre></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The lecture defines fixed-width binning as predefined ranges of equal or deliberately fixed numerical width.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. Custom binning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose a developer-age feature is transformed as:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">0–22   Student\n22–35  Junior\n35–50  Senior\n50–100 Veteran\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>This is best described as:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Quantile binning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Custom/domain-based binning</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> One-hot encoding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Standardization</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>Why can custom bins be preferable to equal-width bins?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> They can reflect meaningful business or domain boundaries</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They always guarantee equal group sizes</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They eliminate missing values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They convert data to text automatically</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. Quantile binning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>What does quartile binning attempt to produce?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Four bins of equal numerical width</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Four bins containing approximately 25% of the observations each</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Four random bins</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Four one-hot columns</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>When is quantile binning especially useful?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Highly uneven/skewed distributions</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Pure image data only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> When every value is identical</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> When categories already have an order</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">9. Log transformation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>Which situation most strongly suggests considering a log transformation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Country = Malaysia/Japan/UK</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Income distribution with a long right tail and a few extremely large values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Colour categories</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Missing text</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>What is the major effect of the log transformation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Expands large values even more</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Compresses large values and reduces right skew</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Converts categories to binary</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Sorts records alphabetically</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">10. Box-Cox</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>What is Box-Cox primarily intended to do?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Encode nominal features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Find a useful power transformation that stabilizes variance and makes the distribution more normal-like</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Detect duplicates</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Generate images</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>What important restriction does the lecture give for Box-Cox?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <div class=\"math-display\">$$x&lt;0$$</div></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <div class=\"math-display\">$$x&gt;0$$</div></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Only integer data</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Only categorical data</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture notes that Box-Cox searches for a parameter $\\lambda$ and requires strictly positive values.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l3p-d13\">PART C — CATEGORICAL FEATURES</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">11. Nominal vs ordinal</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>Which is nominal?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Small, Medium, Large</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Primary, Secondary, University</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Red, Blue, Green</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Low, Medium, High</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>Which is ordinal?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Malaysia, Japan, Brazil</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Cat, Dog, Horse</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Small, Medium, Large</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Red, Blue, Green</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Nominal categories have no meaningful ordering, while ordinal categories do.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">12. Label encoding trap</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"l3_encoder\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Categorical encodings side by side</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Type your own categories and compare label, one-hot, dummy, effect and hashing encodings.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Suppose:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Action    = 0\nAdventure = 1\nSports    = 2\n</code></pre>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>What is the main danger if these are nominal categories?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <p>Model may interpret:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Sports} &gt; \\text{Adventure} &gt; \\text{Action}$$</div></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Model cannot process integers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Values become missing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Dataset becomes too small</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The lecture explicitly warns that numeric labels introduce false magnitude/order when used with nominal categories.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">13. One-hot encoding</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>One-hot encoding of:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Red\nBlue\nGreen\n</code></pre>\n<p>could represent Red as:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>0</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>3</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>[1,0,0]</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>[1,1,1]</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>With $m$ categories, standard one-hot encoding creates:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 1 feature</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> $m-1$ features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> $m$ binary features</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> $2m$ features</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">14. Dummy coding</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>If a variable has 5 categories, dummy coding normally creates:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 5 columns</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 4 columns</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 1 column</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 10 columns</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><div class=\"math-display\">$$m-1=5-1=4$$</div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>Why is one category dropped in dummy coding?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> To provide a reference category and avoid redundant representation in some models</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because the category is invalid</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because pandas cannot store it</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> To remove missing values</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">15. Effect coding</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>How does effect coding differ from dummy coding according to the lecture?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The reference category is represented using −1 rather than all zeros</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It produces images</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It creates $m$ columns</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It uses TF-IDF</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">16. Feature hashing</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q31</span><div><p>A categorical feature has 100,000 unique product IDs.</p>\n<p>Which technique may reduce dimensionality to a fixed number of columns?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> One-hot only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Feature hashing</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Log transform</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Quantile binning</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q32</span><div><p>What is the major trade-off of feature hashing?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Hash collisions</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Mandatory missing values</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It cannot handle categories</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Every category gets its own column</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">17. Critical train/test rule</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"bank-question loose\"><span class=\"qno\">Q33</span><p>Which is correct?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">encoder.fit(X_train)\nX_train_enc = encoder.transform(X_train)\nX_test_enc = encoder.transform(X_test)\n</code></pre>\n<p>or:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">encoder.fit(X_train)\nencoder.fit(X_test)\n</code></pre>\n<p>VISTOK32Z</p></div></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"l3p-d21\">PART D — CODING &amp; REVIEW</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">18. Lecture 3 coding questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><h4>Coding Q1 — Binarization</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.preprocessing import Binarizer\n\nX = [[0], [4], [12], [0]]\n\nb = Binarizer(threshold=0)\nprint(b.fit_transform(X))\n</code></pre>\n<p>Expected result conceptually:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">0\n1\n1\n0\n</code></pre>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Anything above zero becomes active/present.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q2 — Polynomial features</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\n\npoly = PolynomialFeatures(\n    degree=2,\n    include_bias=False\n)\n\nX_new = poly.fit_transform(X)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What is this doing?</p>\n<p>A. Missing-value imputation<br />\nB. Creating original, squared and interaction terms<br />\nC. One-hot encoding<br />\nD. Hashing</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: B</strong></p></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q3 — fixed bins</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;age_group&quot;] = pd.cut(\n    df[&quot;age&quot;],\n    bins=[0, 20, 40, 60, 100]\n)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What happens?</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Continuous ages are mapped into predefined ranges.</p></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q4 — quantile bins</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;income_group&quot;] = pd.qcut(\n    df[&quot;income&quot;],\n    q=4\n)\n</code></pre>\n<p>What does <code>q=4</code> mean?</p>\n<p>A. Width 4<br />\nB. Four quantile groups<br />\nC. Four missing values<br />\nD. Four numeric features</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer: B</strong></p></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q5 — log transform</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">df[&quot;income_log&quot;] = np.log1p(df[&quot;income&quot;])\n</code></pre>\n<p>Why might this be used?</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p><strong>Answer:</strong> To compress large positive values and reduce right skew.</p></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q6 — one-hot encoding</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">pd.get_dummies(\n    df,\n    columns=[&quot;country&quot;]\n)\n</code></pre>\n<p>Given:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">Malaysia\nJapan\nBrazil\n</code></pre>\n<p>the result broadly contains three indicator columns.</p>\n<hr />\n<h4>Coding Q7 — dummy coding</h4>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">pd.get_dummies(\n    df[&quot;country&quot;],\n    drop_first=True\n)\n</code></pre>\n<p>If there are four countries, how many indicator columns?</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$4-1=3$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">19. Lecture 3 answer key</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the answer key</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Q</th>\n<th class=\"ta-c\">Answer</th>\n<th class=\"ta-r\">Q</th>\n<th class=\"ta-c\">Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">1</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">18</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">2</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">19</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">3</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">20</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">4</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">21</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">5</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">22</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">6</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">23</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">7</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">24</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">8</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">25</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">9</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">26</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">10</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">27</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">C</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">11</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">28</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">12</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">29</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">13</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">30</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">14</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">31</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">15</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">32</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">16</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">A</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">33</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">First workflow</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td class=\"ta-r\">17</td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\">B</td>\n<td class=\"ta-r\"></td>\n<td class=\"ta-c\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<hr /></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"l3p-s24\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">20. Lecture 3 traps to drill</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The ones I'd prioritize are:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Nominal vs Ordinal}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Label Encoding vs OneHot}}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Fixed Width vs Quantile Binning}}$$</div>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Log Transformation for strong right skew}}$$</div>\n<p>Also remember:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Fit encoders and similar transformers on <strong>training data</strong>, then reuse them.</p>\n</blockquote></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"l3-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Difficult mode — 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry, coding, hackathon, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the lecture&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"3\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Lecture 3 difficult mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"3\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>A nominal feature has <strong>m = 5</strong> categories. How many columns do one-hot, dummy and effect coding produce?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 5, 4, 4</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 5, 5, 4</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 4, 4, 5</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 1, 4, 5</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>One-hot uses <strong>m</strong> columns. Dummy coding drops a reference level, giving <strong>m − 1</strong>. Effect coding also uses <strong>m − 1</strong>, but codes the reference as −1s instead of 0s. D starts with label encoding's single column, which isn't one-hot.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>What restriction does the lecture give for the Box-Cox transformation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The feature must be categorical.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The values must be integers.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The feature must already be normally distributed.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The values must be strictly positive (x &gt; 0).</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Box-Cox involves $x^\\lambda$ and $\\log x$, which need <strong>x &gt; 0</strong>. A and B are false. C has it backwards — Box-Cox is used to make data <em>more</em> normal-like.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which pandas function creates bins that each hold <strong>roughly the same number of observations</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> <code>pd.qcut</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> <code>pd.cut</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> <code>pd.get_dummies</code></div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> <code>np.log1p</code></div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p><code>qcut</code> makes <strong>quantile</strong> bins (equal counts). <code>cut</code> makes bins from given or equal-width edges. <code>get_dummies</code> one-hot encodes, and <code>log1p</code> is a transform.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>For \"has this user shown any interest in the song?\", why can binarising <code>listen_count</code> beat keeping the raw count?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Binary features always train faster, which improves accuracy.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Raw counts cannot be stored as integers.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The question is about <strong>presence</strong>: 0 → 1 matters a lot, while 37 → 38 is mostly noise, so the raw magnitude adds variation the target doesn't depend on.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Binarisation removes all users who never listened.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Feature engineering means representing what the <strong>problem</strong> needs. If the target depends on whether a user listened at all, the magnitude is irrelevant and only adds noise. A confuses speed with accuracy. B is false. D is wrong — zero-listen users stay as 0.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why does the lecture call feature engineering <strong>iterative</strong>?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because every feature must be recomputed once per epoch.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because model errors reveal what the representation is missing (e.g. weekend mistakes suggest an <code>is_weekend</code> feature), so you engineer, train, evaluate and engineer again.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because polynomial features must be added one degree at a time.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because CRISP-DM forbids changing features after modelling.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The loop is <strong>engineer → train → evaluate → new feature ideas</strong>. Evaluation tells you where the representation fails. A and C are mechanical misreadings, and D is the opposite of CRISP-DM's iterative arrows.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>For a linear model on incomes ranging from RM25,000 to RM1,000,000, why can a log transform help?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It compresses large values far more than small ones, so a few extreme incomes stop dominating the fit, while the order is preserved.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It deletes outliers from the dataset.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It guarantees a perfectly normal distribution.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It lets the model use zero and negative incomes.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>$\\log_{10}$ maps 10 → 1, 1,000 → 3 and 1,000,000 → 6, which pulls the long right tail inward. Rows aren't deleted (B). Normality isn't guaranteed (C). And log is <strong>undefined</strong> at zero and below (D) — that is why <code>log1p</code> or Yeo-Johnson exist.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p><code>PolynomialFeatures(degree=2, include_bias=False)</code> is applied to <strong>3</strong> features. How many output columns?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 6</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 12</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 10</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 9</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>3 originals + 3 squares + 3 pairwise products = <strong>9</strong>. In general this is $\\binom{n+d}{d} - 1 = \\binom{5}{2} - 1 = 9$. C counts the bias column. A forgets the cross terms. This growth is the lecture's \"feature explosion\".</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p><code>pd.get_dummies(df[\"genre\"], drop_first=True)</code> is applied to the categories Action, Adventure and Sports. What does a row with genre <strong>Action</strong> become?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Adventure = 0, Sports = 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Action = 1, Adventure = 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Adventure = −1, Sports = −1</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Adventure = 1, Sports = 0</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p><code>drop_first</code> removes the first category in sorted order, <strong>Action</strong>, which becomes the reference represented by all zeros. C is effect coding. B keeps the dropped column. D is how Adventure is encoded.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>Box-Cox selects <strong>λ = 0</strong> for an income feature. Which transform is applied?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Reciprocal, 1/x</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Natural log, ln(x)</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Square root</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> No change</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Box-Cox is $(x^\\lambda - 1)/\\lambda$ for $\\lambda \\ne 0$ and $\\ln x$ at $\\lambda = 0$. That is why the lecture says Box-Cox can <strong>act like a log transform</strong>. λ = 0.5 gives a square-root-like transform, λ = −1 a reciprocal-like one, and λ = 1 only shifts the values by 1.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A right-skewed income feature is binned two ways:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Counts in the 4 bins</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Fixed-width</td>\n<td>235 · 48 · 14 · 3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Quantile</td>\n<td>75 · 75 · 75 · 75</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Which reading is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Fixed-width is better, because the counts reveal the skew.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both are equivalent, because both have four bins.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Fixed-width spends most bins on the sparse tail; quantile bins give each bin enough data, at the cost of uneven, data-dependent boundaries.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Quantile binning lost information, because the counts are identical.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>That is the lecture's exact trade-off. Equal widths leave tail bins nearly empty on skewed data. Quantile bins <strong>adapt</strong> to the distribution but their edges are no longer round, meaningful numbers. A confuses describing the data with representing it usefully. D misreads equal counts as lost information.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Training data has colours {Red, Blue, Green}; the test batch has {Red, Purple}. You call <code>pd.get_dummies</code> <strong>separately</strong> on each. What goes wrong?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Nothing — get_dummies always returns the same columns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Purple is automatically mapped to Red.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The training data gets an extra Purple column.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The column sets differ (the test set has <code>Purple</code> and lacks <code>Blue</code> and <code>Green</code>), so the model receives a mismatched feature matrix.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Each call learns categories from <strong>its own</strong> data. That is the lecture's critical train-vs-unseen rule. Fit an encoder on training data and reuse it, for example <code>OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\"ignore\")</code>, which maps unseen Purple to all zeros. A, B and C are false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A linear model predicts battle wins from Attack and Defense. It underpredicts when <strong>both</strong> are high and overpredicts when only one is high. What does this pattern suggest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The two features interact; add Attack × Defense.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The features need min-max scaling.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The model is overfitting.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Attack should be binarised.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>An additive model gives each feature a fixed contribution. Errors that depend on the <strong>combination</strong> are the signature of an interaction, which is the lecture's Pokémon example. Scaling (B) doesn't change what an additive model can represent. C doesn't match a systematic pattern. D throws information away.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Education has the levels Primary &lt; Secondary &lt; Diploma &lt; Degree. For a linear model, which encoding is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> One-hot, because label-style encoding is always wrong.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Ordinal mapping (1–4) in the domain order, because the order is real and one column carries it.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Alphabetical label encoding, because it is automatic.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Feature hashing.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture's rule is to ask <strong>nominal or ordinal?</strong> first. For genuinely ordered levels, a domain-defined mapping keeps the order. A over-applies the nominal rule; one-hot would also work but discards the order the model could use. C would order Degree &lt; Diploma &lt; Primary &lt; Secondary, which is meaningless. D is for high cardinality.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A logistic regression must use <code>product_id</code> with 250,000 distinct values on a memory-constrained server. Which choice is most defensible?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> One-hot encoding, because it is exact.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Label encoding, because it uses one column.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Feature hashing into a fixed number of buckets, accepting some collisions.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Drop the feature, because high-cardinality columns are useless.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The lecture introduces hashing for <strong>thousands of categories</strong>: a fixed output size at the cost of collisions. A creates 250k columns. B imposes a false order on IDs, which a linear model treats as magnitude. D may discard strong signal.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Insurance regulations define the age bands 18–25, 26–40, 41–65 and 66+, and premiums must be explained by band. Which binning fits best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Custom bins matching the regulatory bands.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Quantile bins, because they balance the counts.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Fixed-width 10-year bins.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> No binning, because exact age is always better.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The lecture's <strong>custom binning</strong> principle: domain knowledge beats arbitrary mathematics when boundaries carry meaning. Quantile edges (B) would shift with the data and not match the regulation. C ignores the mandated bands. D conflicts with the explanation requirement.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>A dataset has <code>listen_count</code> (only presence matters), <code>income</code> (strongly right-skewed), <code>city</code> (30 unordered values) and <code>size</code> (S &lt; M &lt; L). Which feature design is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Log <code>listen_count</code>; binarise income; ordinal-map city; one-hot size.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Leave everything raw and let the model sort it out.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Binarise <code>listen_count</code>; log-transform <code>income</code>; one-hot <code>city</code>; ordinal-map <code>size</code>.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> One-hot every column, including income.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Each technique matches each column's nature — presence → <strong>binarise</strong>; skew → <strong>log</strong>; nominal → <strong>one-hot</strong>; ordered → <strong>ordinal mapping</strong>. A mismatches every pair. B ignores the point of the lecture. D one-hots a continuous variable, creating thousands of columns.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>You want to know whether a new interaction feature truly helps. Which experiment is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Add it and check whether <strong>training</strong> accuracy improves.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Add it, retrain, and compare performance on held-out validation data against the same model without it; keep it only if it generalises better.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Add it only if it has the largest coefficient.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Add every possible interaction at once and keep the model with the highest training score.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>This is the iterative loop, judged on <strong>unseen data</strong>. A and D reward fitting more, which a new feature almost always does on training data — the overfitting trap. C confuses coefficient size, which depends on scale, with predictive value.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Design categorical encoding that won't break when production sends a category never seen in training.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Call <code>pd.get_dummies</code> on each production batch.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Fit <code>OneHotEncoder(handle_unknown=\"ignore\")</code> on training data, save it with the model, and only <code>transform</code> test and production data.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Refit the encoder on each production batch so it always knows every category.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Label-encode in production, because unseen categories get the next integer.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Fit once on training data, reuse everywhere — the lecture's critical rule, plus explicit handling of unseen values. A and C re-learn the categories, so the columns differ from training. D gives an unseen category a number the model never learned, with a fake order.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>In \"entity embeddings\", a neural network learns a dense vector for each category (for example each of 5,000 stores) during training. What advantage do they have over one-hot encoding?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Similar categories can learn nearby vectors, sharing statistical strength in a low-dimensional space, instead of 5,000 independent, equally distant columns.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> They need no training data.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> They guarantee an ordinal order.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> They remove the need for a train/test split.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>One-hot makes every category <strong>equally different</strong> from every other — the same limitation Lecture 4 shows for words. Learned embeddings place behaviourally similar stores close together, which helps rare categories. It is the categorical version of word embeddings. B, C and D are false.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>100 distinct categories are hashed uniformly into 1,000 buckets. Approximately what is the probability that <strong>at least one</strong> pair collides?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> About 0.10</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> About 0.50</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 0</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> About 0.99</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>This is the birthday problem: $P(\\text{no collision}) = \\prod_{i=0}^{99}(1 - i/1000) \\approx e^{-100 \\cdot 99 / 2000} \\approx 0.006$, so $P(\\ge 1) \\approx 0.994$. On average about $\\binom{100}{2}/1000 \\approx 5$ pairs collide. Collisions are near-certain long before buckets run out, which is the lecture's trade-off made concrete.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Coding</span></div><p>How many columns does this produce?</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">from sklearn.preprocessing import PolynomialFeatures\nPolynomialFeatures(degree=3, include_bias=False).fit_transform(X)  # X has 10 columns\n</code></pre></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> 30</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> 1,000</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> 285</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> 165</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The number of monomials of degree 1–3 in 10 variables is $\\binom{10+3}{3} - 1 = 286 - 1 = 285$. A counts only powers of single variables. B is $10^3$, which counts ordered products. D is wrong because degree-1 and degree-2 terms are also included. Going from 10 to 285 features is the lecture's <strong>feature explosion</strong>.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Hackathon</span></div><p>In a hackathon, you replace <code>merchant_id</code> with the <strong>mean fraud rate of that merchant over the whole training set</strong>. CV shows 0.98, the leaderboard 0.71. Why?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Merchant IDs have too few categories.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Target encoding must use the median.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Each row's encoding included its own label, which leaked the target; for rare merchants it nearly equals the label. Use out-of-fold target encoding with smoothing.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The leaderboard uses a different metric.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Target encoding computed on the same rows it encodes <strong>contains the answer</strong>. Out-of-fold encoding computes each row's value from other folds only, and smoothing $\\frac{n\\bar y + m\\,p}{n+m}$ pulls rare categories toward the prior $p$. It's the lecture's \"fit only on what you're allowed to see\" rule applied <em>within</em> the training data.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>Quantile income bins were fitted on last year's training data. This year, nominal incomes rise about 30%, and most customers now fall into the top bin. What is happening?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Quantile bins re-adapt automatically at prediction time.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Fitted bin edges are fixed parameters. Under distribution shift they stop splitting the population evenly, so drift must be monitored and the bins (and model) refitted.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The bins are fine; only the model is wrong.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Fixed-width bins would be immune.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Quantile edges are <strong>learned from training data</strong> and then reused, which is the consistency rule. When the distribution shifts, reused edges no longer represent quartiles. A is the trap — transform doesn't refit. D is false; any fixed edges suffer under shift.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>A true risk relationship is smooth in age. An analyst bins age into 5 bins before fitting. What is the main statistical cost?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> None — binning always reduces variance with no downside.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Within-bin variation is discarded, and the model becomes a step function with artificial jumps at the bin edges, losing information and power.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It increases the number of features exponentially.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It makes the feature nominal, so ordering is lost.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The lecture says binning trades precision for abstraction. The extension is the <em>price</em>: a step function forces people at 39.9 and 40.1 into different risk levels, and ignores the gradient inside each bin. Binning helps when the boundaries are meaningful (D15) or the relationship really is stepwise. A is false. C confuses binning with polynomial expansion. D is false — bins can stay ordinal.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>The true target is $\\text{Attack} \\times \\text{Defense}$. Which model <strong>cannot</strong> capture it without an explicit interaction feature?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> A deep decision tree.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A linear regression on Attack and Defense only.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A neural network with hidden layers.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A gradient-boosted ensemble of depth-6 trees.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>A linear model is <strong>additive</strong> in its inputs, so it can't represent a product unless you engineer the column — which is exactly why the lecture creates Attack × Defense. Trees with depth ≥ 2 and neural networks can learn interactions themselves (A, C, D). An ensemble of depth-1 stumps, by contrast, would also be additive.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p><code>monthly_profit</code> is right-skewed but contains zeros and negative values. Which transformation is most appropriate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Box-Cox, because it chooses λ automatically.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Yeo-Johnson, a power-transform family defined for all real values, with λ chosen by maximum likelihood.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Plain log transform.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Binarisation, because skewed data must be binned.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Box-Cox requires <strong>x &gt; 0</strong>, which is the lecture's stated restriction, so A fails. Log fails at zero and below (C). Yeo-Johnson extends the Box-Cox idea to zero and negative values. D throws away magnitude unnecessarily.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>Label-encoding a nominal feature is \"dangerous\" in the lecture. How dangerous is it for a <strong>gradient-boosted tree</strong> compared with a <strong>linear</strong> model?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Equally harmful for both, because both read the codes as magnitudes.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Harmless for both.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Much less harmful for trees: they split on thresholds and can isolate categories with several splits, though arbitrary codes can need more splits. A linear model treats the codes as true magnitudes.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> More harmful for trees than for linear models.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The danger is the <strong>implied magnitude and order</strong>. Linear models multiply the code by a weight, so the danger is direct. Trees only use order to place thresholds, so arbitrary codes cost extra splits but don't impose a linear effect. That's why many boosting libraries accept categorical codes directly. The lecture's rule is still the safe default for linear and distance-based models.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>A ridge regression (L2-regularised, with an intercept) uses <strong>full</strong> one-hot encoding of a 4-level feature, without dropping a column. Which statement is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It fails because the matrix is singular.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It is acceptable: regularisation makes the system solvable despite the collinearity, and all levels are treated symmetrically. Ordinary least squares without regularisation is where dummy coding matters most.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Effect coding is required for any regularised model.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Dummy coding must always be used, regardless of the model.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The dummy-variable trap is <strong>perfect collinearity</strong> between the one-hot columns and the intercept. Unregularised OLS then has no unique solution, which is why the lecture motivates dummy coding. An L2 penalty adds $\\lambda I$, making the system invertible, so full one-hot is common with regularised models. A ignores the penalty. C and D are overgeneralisations.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A regulator requires that, all else equal, a <strong>higher income can never lower</strong> the predicted credit approval. Which design meets this?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Log-transform income; that guarantees monotonicity.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Quantile-bin income into 10 bins.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Drop income from the model.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Train a gradient-boosted model with a monotonic (increasing) constraint on income, and verify it with partial-dependence checks.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Monotonic constraints restrict every tree split so the effect of income can only increase, which enforces domain knowledge directly. A keeps the order of the feature, but the <em>model</em> can still learn a non-monotonic response. B can produce non-monotonic bin effects. C loses predictive power. It's feature engineering's \"domain knowledge\" principle turned into a model constraint.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Hackathon</span></div><p><code>device_model</code> has 3,000 values; 2,600 of them appear fewer than 10 times, and the test set will contain unseen models. Which plan is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> One-hot all 3,000 values.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Drop the rows with rare devices.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> On the training data, map categories with fewer than 10 occurrences to <code>\"Other\"</code>; fit the encoder on that; at prediction time, map unseen values to <code>\"Other\"</code> as well.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Label-encode in order of first appearance.</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Grouping rare levels shrinks the number of columns, reduces overfitting on tiny categories, and gives unseen values a safe home. Learning the threshold from <strong>train only</strong> keeps it consistent. A explodes in width and overfits. B deletes data and fails on unseen values anyway. D imposes a false order.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
